380:                                                                                                                                                       Preface
381:
382:Architecture versions and variants
383:
384:              The ARM instruction set architecture has evolved significantly since it was first developed, and will
385:              continue to be developed in the future. Six major versions of the instruction set have been defined to date,
386:              denoted by the version numbers 1 to 6. Of these, the first three versions including the original 26-bit
387:              architecture (the 32-bit architecture was introduced at ARMv3) are now OBSOLETE. All bits and encodings
388:              that were used for 26-bit features become RESERVED for future expansion by ARM Ltd.
389:
390:              Versions can be qualified with variant letters to specify collections of additional instructions that are
391:              included as an architecture extension. Extensions are typically included in the base architecture of the next
392:              version number, ARMv5T being the notable exception. Provision is also made to exclude variants by
393:              prefixing the variant letter with x, for example the xP variant described below in the summary of version 5
394:              features.
395:
396:                      Note
397:              The xM variant which indicates that long multiplies (32 x 32 multiplies with 64-bit results) are not
398:              supported, has been withdrawn.
399:
400:              The valid architecture variants are as follows (variant in brackets for legacy reasons only):
401:
402:              ARMv4, ARMv4T, ARMv5T, (ARMv5TExP), ARMv5TE, ARMv5TEJ, and ARMv6
403:
404:              The following architecture variants are now OBSOLETE:
405:
406:              ARMv1, ARMv2, ARMv2a, ARMv3, ARMv3G, ARMv3M, ARMv4xM, ARMv4TxM, ARMv5,
407:              ARMv5xM, and ARMv5TxM
408:
409:              Details on OBSOLETE versions are available on request from ARM.
410:

